//~ compile_fail(backend)
//~ error[KORU030]: Resource 'shared' obligation <reading!> was not discharged

// NEGATIVE (must FAIL to compile): take the read lock and never give it back.
//
// This one is worth its own file because of what it is NOT. `unikraft/alloc`
// deliberately does not claim "forgetting to free is a compile error", and its
// README says why: a dropped `<live!>` block has exactly one unattended terminal
// disposer, so the compiler INSERTS the `free` and reports nothing. Correct
// behaviour, but an insertion is not a catch, and stating it as one would be a
// claim the module cannot support.
//
// `unikraft/lock` CAN make the claim, and it is the same mechanism reaching the
// opposite outcome rather than a different mechanism. `<reading>`'s only
// consumer is `read.release`, and `read.release` is not a disposal — it mints
// `<idle!>`, another live obligation. There is therefore nothing for
// auto-discharge to elect, and a dropped hold is reported:
//
//     error[KORU030]: Resource 'shared' obligation <reading!> was not
//                     discharged. Call: read.release
//
// The three-way split, stated exactly, because the differences are the point:
//
//   * forget to UNLOCK          -> compile ERROR (this file; `<writing!>` the same)
//   * forget to USE a new lock  -> compile ERROR (negative_destroy_without_hold.kz)
//   * forget to DESTROY an idle lock -> compile-time INSERTION, silent, no leak
//
// The C's position: a held rwlock that is never released is not a memory error
// and no assertion in `uklock` can see it. `rwlock.c` has nothing to check —
// from inside the lock, a critical section that has not ended yet and one that
// will never end are the same state. It shows up as the NEXT acquirer hanging,
// in a different thread, with a stack that does not mention the function that
// forgot.
//
// EXPECTED: error[KORU030]: Resource 'shared' obligation <reading!> was not
//           discharged. Call: read.release

// The `unikraft` namespace is declared here, not inherited: koruc's built-in
// alias probes the sibling koru-libs CHECKOUT, so in a worktree this file would
// otherwise compile against main's lifts instead of the ones beside it.
~std/compiler:paths {
    unikraft: {{ ENTRY }}/../..
}

~import unikraft/lock
~import std/io

~unikraft/lock:probe
| ready proof |> unikraft/lock:create(proof.locks)
    | lock guard |> unikraft/lock:read.acquire(lock: guard): shared
        |> std/io:print.ln("the critical section, and then nothing gives the lock back")
    | unavailable why |> std/io:print.ln("create failed: {{ why:s }}")
| unsupported why |> std/io:print.ln("uk_rwlock layout refused: {{ why:s }}")
